# Apex Lifespan Cursor Rules

Work in hobby-project mode: read only what matters, make the useful product change, run narrow checks, and summarize plainly.

## Active Context

- Read `AGENTS.md` and `docs/codex/project.md` first.
- Use `docs/codex/roadmap.md` only for roadmap, prioritization, or choosing next product work.
- Read `docs/codex/handoff.md` only for resume/current dirty-tree state.
- **Local PostgreSQL is the development source of truth** (`APEX_DATA_SOURCE=database`). `seed-data.ts` is fallback only.
- Do not seed or mutate preview/production databases unless the user explicitly asks to promote local work.
- Current active docs override old handoffs, archived plans, generated logs, `.ai/delegation/`, legacy checklist/runbook/workflow docs, and reference command catalogs.

## What To Build

- Prefer visible public product improvements: evidence map usefulness, intervention detail pages, traceable coverage, AU/TGA caveats, uncertainty/readability, search/filtering, and privacy-safe aggregate signals.
- If a task mainly creates readiness gates, queues, promotion chains, Composer monitoring, review packets, launch rehearsals, roadmap bookkeeping, or command catalogs, do not do it unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not recreate removed npm scripts or process chains.

## Hard Stops

Ask the user first before production deploys, **preview/production database promotion**, database migration on remote envs, secrets, destructive actions, dependency/security changes, or medical/regulatory boundary changes.

Use `Human reviewed` only after explicit human confirmation. Do not infer product-level ARTG/AUST status from generic intervention evidence. Do not provide peptide sourcing, compounding, reconstitution, injection, cycling, dosing, or self-administration guidance.

## Dirty Worktree

The worktree may contain unrelated dirty files. Do not clean, revert, stage, commit, push, or delete unrelated work unless the user explicitly asks.

## Checks

Use the smallest useful check for the changed surface. Common commands are `npm run test`, `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm run build`, `npm run db:validate`, and `npm run db:generate`.
